    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.clique_number - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.clique_number.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.clique_number()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get the clique number (size of the maximum clique).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.clique_number()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get the clique number (size of the maximum clique).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.clique_number()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get the clique number (size of the maximum clique).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet the clique number (size of the maximum clique).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L40094-L40204){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.clique_number()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Clique number](../assets/graphs/clique-number.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A five-node graph whose largest cliques have three nodes. One of them, A B C, is drawn in the accent color; B C D ties it, and no four nodes are all linked. The card gives the clique number 3.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe clique number `ω(G)` is the size of the largest clique in the graph, where a clique is\na set of nodes that are all directly connected to one another. This is a fundamental graph\ninvariant used in graph theory and combinatorial optimization.\n\nIt is a compact summary of how tightly knit the densest part of a network is. Typical uses:\n\n- comparing the \"core tightness\" of several networks with a single number\n- giving a lower bound on the chromatic number (any coloring needs at least `ω(G)`\n  colors), which matters for scheduling and register-allocation style problems\n- quickly checking whether a graph contains a triangle (`ω(G) >= 3`) or a larger\n  complete subgraph\n\nThe value equals `len(graph.maximum_clique())`; use `maximum_clique()` if you also need to know\nwhich nodes form the clique.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Size of the maximum clique. Returns `0` for empty graphs and `1` for graphs that have\n    nodes but no edges.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If called on a directed graph.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe clique number $\\omega(G)$ is one of the fundamental graph invariants and is\n$\\mathsf{NP}$-hard to compute in general. Extremes are easy to reason about, though:\nfor the complete graph $\\omega(K_n) = n$; for any bipartite graph $\\omega(G) \\le 2$,\nbecause a third vertex would need to be adjacent to two already-adjacent vertices,\ncontradicting bipartiteness; and for any triangle-free graph the same $\\omega(G)\n\\le 2$ bound holds. The implementation shares the Bron-Kerbosch enumeration behind\n`find_cliques()` and inherits its $O(3^{n/3})$ worst-case bound. Undirected graphs\nonly.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~maximum_clique`) \n\n:   The nodes of a largest clique.\n\n[](`~node_clique_number`) \n\n:   The size of the largest clique at each node.\n\n[](`~chromatic_number_upper_bound`) \n\n:   An upper bound on the colors needed.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a square with one diagonal, the diagonal closes two triangles, but no four nodes are\nall linked, so the clique number is 3:\n\n::: {#0b38c089 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"A\"), (\"A\", \"C\")]\n)\ngraph.clique_number()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n3\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Comparing networks\n\nThe clique number of a social network is the size of its tightest group. In the karate\nclub and among the dolphins, it's 5. Among college football teams it's 9, nine teams that\nall played one another, and in *Les Misérables* it's 10, characters who all meet at the\nbarricade:\n\n::: {#175f35f0 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n{\n    name: getattr(ec, name)().clique_number()\n    for name in [\"karate_club\", \"dolphins\", \"football\", \"les_miserables\"]\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n{'karate_club': 5, 'dolphins': 5, 'football': 9, 'les_miserables': 10}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe nine football teams are the Atlantic Coast Conference, where every team played every\nother:\n\n::: {#78829064 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfootball = ec.football()\nclique = football.maximum_clique()\n\n{football.get_node(n).attributes[\"conference\"] for n in clique}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n{'Atlantic Coast'}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A lower bound on colors\n\nEvery node of a clique needs its own color, so a graph needs at least as many colors as\nits clique number. `exams()` joins two of a made-up college's courses when some student\ntakes both, so their exams can't share a slot. Its clique number is 4, and DSATUR finds a\ntimetable with 4 slots, so four is the fewest:\n\n::: {#63af56ba .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\nslots = exams.greedy_color(strategy=\"dsatur\")\n\nexams.clique_number(), len(set(slots.values()))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(4, 4)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nCalculus, Physics, Chemistry and Biology, the clique, take one slot each:\n\n::: {#e69ce6f6 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams.set_node_attrs(\"slot\", slots).plot(\n    color_by=\"slot\", size_by=\"students\", label_by=\"label\", seed=3,\n    height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not